<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="Working-With-IMAP" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient</codeEntityReference> provides access to messages
        stored on an IMAP server. Unlike POP3, IMAP is designed for leaving messages on the server: it supports
        multiple folders, message flags and keywords, server-side searching and sorting, partial downloads and
        notifications of changes made by other clients.
      </para>
      <para>
        For information about connecting securely, logging and error handling, see
        <link xlink:href="Connecting-To-A-Server"/>. For information about authenticating, see
        <link xlink:href="Authentication"/>.
      </para>
      <autoOutline />
    </introduction>

    <section address="Capabilities">
      <title>Server capabilities</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          IMAP has a large number of extensions, and servers vary widely in which ones they support. After
          connecting (and again after authenticating, since many servers advertise additional capabilities
          once the user has logged in), the <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient.Capabilities</codeEntityReference>
          property lists the extensions supported by the server. MailKit uses these extensions automatically
          where possible, but some features (such as sorting, threading or <literal>QRESYNC</literal>) are only
          available when the server supports them.
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapExamples.cs" region="Capabilities"/>
        <para>
          Many folder-level features can also be checked using
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">M:MailKit.IMailFolder.Supports(MailKit.FolderFeature)</codeEntityReference>.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Folders">
      <title>Folders</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          The <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient.Inbox</codeEntityReference> folder is always
          available. Other folders live inside one or more namespaces. Most servers have a single personal
          namespace, but some also have namespaces for folders that are shared with other users or are public:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapExamples.cs" region="Namespaces"/>
        <para>
          You can list all of the folders in a namespace at once (optionally requesting status information such
          as the message count for each folder in the same round-trip), or walk the folder hierarchy one level
          at a time:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="ListFolders"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="SpecialFolders">
      <title>Special folders</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Folder names such as "Sent", "Drafts" or "Trash" vary between servers and are often localized. If the
          server supports the <literal>SPECIAL-USE</literal> (or Gmail's <literal>XLIST</literal>) extension,
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.IMailStore.GetFolder(MailKit.SpecialFolder)</codeEntityReference> can
          be used to locate them regardless of their names:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="SpecialFolders"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="ManagingFolders">
      <title>Creating, renaming and deleting folders</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Folders can be created, renamed, deleted, subscribed to and unsubscribed from.
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.Status</codeEntityReference> can be
          used to get information such as the message count or unread count of a folder without opening it:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="ManageFolders"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="OpeningFolders">
      <title>Opening folders</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Before you can access the messages in a folder, it must be opened using
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.Open</codeEntityReference>. Only one
          folder can be open at a time per connection; opening a folder implicitly closes the previously opened
          folder.
        </para>
        <list class="bullet">
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeInline>FolderAccess.ReadOnly</codeInline> opens the folder without allowing changes. Use this
              when you only need to read messages.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeInline>FolderAccess.ReadWrite</codeInline> is required to change flags, append, expunge or
              move messages out of the folder.
            </para>
          </listItem>
        </list>
        <para>
          Once open, properties such as <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.Count</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.UidValidity</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.UidNext</codeEntityReference> and
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.PermanentFlags</codeEntityReference> are populated and
          kept up-to-date as the server sends notifications.
        </para>
        <alert class="note">
          <para>
            MailKit never implicitly marks messages as <literal>\Seen</literal> when downloading them, even in a
            folder opened with <codeInline>FolderAccess.ReadWrite</codeInline>. To mark a message as read, add the
            <literal>\Seen</literal> flag explicitly (see <link xlink:href="#Flags">Flags and keywords</link>).
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="MessageIdentifiers">
      <title>Indexes and unique identifiers</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Messages in an open folder can be referred to in two ways:
        </para>
        <list class="bullet">
          <listItem>
            <para>
              By <legacyItalic>index</legacyItalic> (a zero-based sequence number). Indexes shift whenever a
              message is expunged, so they should only be used briefly and never stored.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              By <legacyItalic>unique identifier</legacyItalic> (a <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.UniqueId</codeEntityReference>).
              A message's UID never changes as long as the folder's
              <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.UidValidity</codeEntityReference> stays the same, so
              UIDs are suitable for persisting between sessions.
            </para>
          </listItem>
        </list>
        <para>
          Most APIs have overloads that accept either. Prefer UIDs wherever possible.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="FetchingSummaries">
      <title>Fetching message summaries</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Downloading entire messages just to display a message list is slow and wasteful. Instead, use
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.Fetch</codeEntityReference> to
          request only the information you need, such as the envelope (subject, addresses, date), flags, size,
          a short preview of the text or the MIME structure of the message. The results are returned as
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.IMessageSummary</codeEntityReference> objects:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="FetchSummaries"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="DownloadingMessages">
      <title>Downloading messages</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Use <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.GetMessage</codeEntityReference>
          to download and parse an entire message:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapExamples.cs" region="DownloadMessagesByUniqueId"/>
        <para>
          To save messages to disk without parsing them (or to process very large messages without loading them
          entirely into memory), use <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.GetStream</codeEntityReference>:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapExamples.cs" region="DownloadMessageStreamsByUniqueId"/>
        <para>
          If you have fetched the <codeInline>BodyStructure</codeInline> of a message, you can download
          individual parts (such as only the text body, or a single attachment) using
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.GetBodyPart</codeEntityReference>:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapBodyPartExamples.cs" region="GetBodyPartsByUniqueId"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Flags">
      <title>Flags and keywords</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Each message has a set of system flags (<codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.MessageFlags</codeEntityReference>,
          such as <literal>\Seen</literal>, <literal>\Flagged</literal>, <literal>\Answered</literal> and
          <literal>\Deleted</literal>) and may have any number of user-defined keywords (if the folder's
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.PermanentFlags</codeEntityReference> include
          <codeInline>MessageFlags.UserDefined</codeInline>). Flags can be added, removed or replaced:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="Flags"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Searching">
      <title>Searching and sorting</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Searching is performed on the server using a <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Search.SearchQuery</codeEntityReference>,
          which can be combined using <codeInline>And</codeInline>, <codeInline>Or</codeInline> and
          <codeInline>Not</codeInline>. The result is a list of UIDs that can then be passed to
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.Fetch</codeEntityReference> or
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.GetMessage</codeEntityReference>:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="Search"/>
        <para>
          If the server supports the <literal>SORT</literal> extension, results can also be sorted on the server.
          Otherwise, fetch the summaries and sort them on the client using
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.MessageSorter.Sort</codeEntityReference>:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="Sort"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="AppendCopyMove">
      <title>Appending, copying and moving messages</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.Append</codeEntityReference>
          uploads a message into a folder (for example, to save a copy of a sent message in the Sent folder).
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.CopyTo</codeEntityReference> and
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.MoveTo</codeEntityReference>
          copy or move messages between folders. If the server supports the <literal>UIDPLUS</literal> extension,
          these methods return the UIDs of the new messages in the destination folder.
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="AppendCopyMove"/>
        <alert class="note">
          <para>
            If the server does not support the <literal>MOVE</literal> extension, MailKit emulates
            <codeInline>MoveTo</codeInline> by copying the messages, marking the originals as
            <literal>\Deleted</literal> and expunging them.
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="DeletingMessages">
      <title>Deleting messages</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Deleting messages in IMAP is a two-step process: messages are first marked with the
          <literal>\Deleted</literal> flag and are then permanently removed when the folder is
          <legacyItalic>expunged</legacyItalic>. Calling
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.Expunge</codeEntityReference>
          with no arguments removes <legacyItalic>all</legacyItalic> messages marked as deleted, including any that
          were marked by other clients. If the server supports <literal>UIDPLUS</literal>, you can expunge only
          specific messages.
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapFolderExamples.cs" region="DeleteAndExpunge"/>
        <para>
          Many clients (and servers such as Gmail) treat "delete" as "move to Trash" instead. In that case, use
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.IMailFolder.MoveTo</codeEntityReference> with the
          <codeInline>SpecialFolder.Trash</codeInline> folder.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Synchronizing">
      <title>Synchronizing a local cache</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Applications that display a mailbox (rather than just processing new messages) usually keep a local
          cache of message summaries so that they do not need to re-download everything each time they connect.
          Keeping the cache in sync with the server relies on three values:
        </para>
        <table>
          <tableHeader>
            <row>
              <entry><para>Value</para></entry>
              <entry><para>Purpose</para></entry>
            </row>
          </tableHeader>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.UidValidity</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                Identifies the "generation" of UIDs in the folder. If it ever changes (for example, because the
                folder was deleted and re-created), every cached UID is invalid and the cache must be discarded.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.UidNext</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                The UID that will be assigned to the next message added to the folder. Any message with a UID
                greater than or equal to the value saved during the previous sync is new.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailFolder.HighestModSeq</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                Only available if the server supports <literal>CONDSTORE</literal>. Every change to a message's
                flags increments the folder's mod-sequence, making it possible to ask for only the messages that
                have changed since the previous sync.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </table>
        <para>
          At a minimum, the cache needs to store these values for each folder along with the UID and any other
          information you display for each message:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapSyncExamples.cs" region="CacheState"/>
        <para>
          New messages can be fetched by requesting the range of UIDs starting at the previously saved
          <literal>UIDNEXT</literal> value:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapSyncExamples.cs" region="FetchNewMessages"/>
        <para>
          <legacyBold>Basic synchronization.</legacyBold> Without any extensions, the client must check the
          <literal>UIDVALIDITY</literal>, search for all of the UIDs in the folder to find which cached messages
          have been expunged, re-fetch the flags for every cached message and then fetch any new messages. This
          works with every server, but the cost grows with the size of the folder:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapSyncExamples.cs" region="BasicSync"/>
        <para>
          <legacyBold>CONDSTORE.</legacyBold> If the server supports the <literal>CONDSTORE</literal> extension,
          the client can use a <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.FetchRequest</codeEntityReference> with
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.FetchRequest.ChangedSince</codeEntityReference> set to the previously
          saved <literal>HIGHESTMODSEQ</literal> so that only the messages whose flags have actually changed are
          returned:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapSyncExamples.cs" region="CondStoreSync"/>
        <para>
          <legacyBold>QRESYNC.</legacyBold> The <literal>QRESYNC</literal> extension goes further: by passing the
          saved <literal>UIDVALIDITY</literal>, <literal>HIGHESTMODSEQ</literal> and known UIDs when opening the
          folder, the server reports both expunged messages (via the
          <codeEntityReference>E:MailKit.IMailFolder.MessagesVanished</codeEntityReference> event) and changed
          flags (via the <codeEntityReference>E:MailKit.IMailFolder.MessageFlagsChanged</codeEntityReference>
          event) as part of opening the folder, so that no searching is needed. <literal>QRESYNC</literal> must be
          enabled once per connection using
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient.Enable</codeEntityReference>
          before any folders are opened:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapSyncExamples.cs" region="QuickResyncSync"/>
        <alert class="tip">
          <para>
            While a folder is open, keep the cache up-to-date by handling the
            <codeEntityReference>E:MailKit.IMailFolder.CountChanged</codeEntityReference>,
            <codeEntityReference>E:MailKit.IMailFolder.MessageExpunged</codeEntityReference> (or
            <codeEntityReference>E:MailKit.IMailFolder.MessagesVanished</codeEntityReference> when
            <literal>QRESYNC</literal> is enabled) and
            <codeEntityReference>E:MailKit.IMailFolder.MessageFlagsChanged</codeEntityReference> events, and save
            the <literal>UIDNEXT</literal> and <literal>HIGHESTMODSEQ</literal> values when you are done.
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Idle">
      <title>Receiving notifications with IDLE</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          IMAP servers only send notifications about changes (new messages, expunged messages, flag changes)
          in response to a command. To be notified immediately, use the <literal>IDLE</literal> extension,
          which allows the server to push notifications to the client while it waits.
        </para>
        <para>
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient.CreateIdleSession(MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapIdleOptions)</codeEntityReference>
          creates an <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapIdleSession</codeEntityReference> that manages
          the details for you: it automatically re-issues the <literal>IDLE</literal> command before the server
          times it out, falls back to polling with <literal>NOOP</literal> if the server does not support
          <literal>IDLE</literal>, and coalesces bursts of notifications. The following example monitors the
          Inbox for new messages:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapIdleSessionExample.cs"/>
        <alert class="note">
          <para>
            The session ends the <literal>IDLE</literal> command before returning each batch of changes, so the
            same connection can be used to fetch the new messages before waiting again. However, no other
            commands can be sent while the session is waiting; use a separate
            <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient</codeEntityReference> if you need to perform other
            operations concurrently.
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <relatedTopics>
      <link xlink:href="Connecting-To-A-Server"/>
      <link xlink:href="Authentication"/>
      <link xlink:href="Retrieving-Messages-With-POP3"/>
      <link xlink:href="Parsing-Messages"/>
    </relatedTopics>
  </developerConceptualDocument>
</topic>
